  "details": "Summary\n\nMy avatar uses Native Face Tracking with Eye Gaze Mode set to \"Bone Rotation.\" With Selfie Expression (webcam), the eyes move through the full configured range. With tracking through VRCFT, the eye movement range becomes extremely narrow.\n\nEnvironment\n\n・VRChat build: 1953\n\n・VRCFT version: 6.0.0\n\n・Modules / devices: iPhone, Quest Pro, Bigscreen Beyond, Virtual Desktop\n\n・Platform: PC (Windows)\n\nAvatar setup\n\n・Eye Gaze Mode: Bone Rotation\n\n > Horizontal (left/right): 6°\n\n > Vertical (up/down): 4°\n\n・Face Tracking: \"Use All Tracking Keys\" enabled\n\n・Native parameters connected to the Animator Controller parameters via Parameter Behaviors\n\n・The face tracking itself is driven by the Animator Controller\n\nSteps to reproduce\n\n1. Use an avatar with the setup above (avtr_66029737-50fe-4e4c-b330-d456bbc57b90)\n\n2. Start VRCFT with eye tracking enabled\n\n3. Look far left/right and up/down\n\nExpected behavior\n\nThe eyes rotate through the configured range (6° horizontal, 4° vertical), the same as with Selfie Expression.\n\nActual behavior\n\nThe eye rotation range is far smaller than configured (see attached video).\n\nIsolation results\n\n・Selfie Expression (webcam) + Native Face Tracking (Bone Rotation): works correctly\n\n・Older OSC-based face tracking (before native support) + VRCFT: works correctly\n\n・A different avatar without Native Face Tracking (avtr_2988d5c9-f42a-4cf9-bf13-4175589d52d5) + VRCFT: works correctly\n\n・Native Face Tracking (Bone Rotation) + VRCFT: range is reduced\n\nAs far as I can tell, the issue only happens with this combination of Native Face Tracking (Bone Rotation) and VRCFT.\n\nTest avatars\n\n・Native Face Tracking avatar: avtr_66029737-50fe-4e4c-b330-d456bbc57b90\n\n・VRCFT (OSC) face tracking avatar, for comparison: avtr_2988d5c9-f42a-4cf9-bf13-4175589d52d5",
